#edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =5k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $5k-5\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329822 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329822 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329822 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329822 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the unique solution of $Ax=0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does not exist" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329822 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffbb2a4c-595d-59cc-bffd-a39a18b105b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329822 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412408 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412408 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412408 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "023017fb-407e-5bef-96fe-5ca4f9043072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412409 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412409 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412409 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d7b32a8-3cec-559f-a17c-bf7b28c6fbce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412410 #edb/ref 1080 "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412410 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412410 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "236e5192-a40c-577c-b850-ee910be00f48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412411 #edb/ref 1080 "k=-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412411 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412411 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2fd3908a-3f72-5df0-977b-949624bbea37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412412 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412412 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "578d705a-4c53-5798-b6ab-04cb9c9c2712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412413 #edb/ref 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412413 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412413 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ad20df4f-b7fd-5955-aa00-9373ab0ec236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412414 #edb/ref 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412414 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412414 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bffc8d55-ee5e-5f8f-9215-8f1f05d7db3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412415 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412415 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412415 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cb491f25-cc92-5958-9889-8d4238a0075e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412416 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412416 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412416 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de2cfeee-36d6-5198-8124-8b5f27baa495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412417 #edb/ref 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is not invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412417 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412417 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa69be2d-b4be-5af7-a14e-91a403fff329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412418 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412418 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412418 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412418 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6af571c7-96ee-5d86-b8e5-c8021a9767c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412419 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412419 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412419 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412419 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bcbfa28f-c9f4-5861-a817-67e808342830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412420 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412420 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the lines $x+y=3$ and $x-ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at exactly one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12b35a91-483f-55c8-a96f-b8126a049cff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412420 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $x+y=3$ and $x-ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nFinally, notice that if $k$ can't be equal to $-1,$ since  $det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -(-1)\\end{aligned}]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*f the lines intersect at exactly one point, then $det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412421 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412421 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412421 #edb/ref 1071 "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The lines $5x-4y=0$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79c14c2f-568d-5ff3-b628-6c8dd8d0639f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412421 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of  the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
